On Thu, 29 Oct 2026 at 18:42 +0800, Zijun Hu wrote:
> QCA9377 is not ROME(QCA6174A), btusb driver does not support it,
> linux-firmware repo also does not have firmware it.
>
> See table @qca_devices_table for supported controllers from ROME.

Thank you. Please let me explain what I am asking and why.

The problem: this laptop's QCA9377 module (USB 13d3:3503) had no btusb
entry, so its controller ran the ROM firmware. During mSBC (HFP
wideband) calls it stopped answering HCI commands, and only a power-off
brought it back. dc16388d45ec gives the ID BTUSB_QCA_ROME, so btusb
now loads qca/rampatch_usb_00000302.bin and qca/nvm_usb_00000302.bin.
With that setup, in 74 SCO links, 50 of them mSBC, the hang has not
occurred.

On the table you pointed to: the controller answers the version
request with rom_version 0x00000302, which qca_devices_table lists as
"Rome 3.2". So btusb treats its Bluetooth part as ROME 3.2, and it
works with the ROME 3.2 files.

What would help, and what each answer would change:

1. Is the ROME 3.2 rampatch and NVM the right setup for these QCA9377
   modules? btusb already gives it to three: 04ca:3015, 0cf3:e500 and
   13d3:3503. If it is right, those entries stand and new IDs can be
   added with confidence. If not, what should they load instead?

2. Is there a list of USB IDs of modules with this ROM, or a way to
   recognise them? Today each ID is added only after a user runs into
   the problem; three were added this year. A list would let them be
   added at once.

3. Is the hang a known issue of ROM build 0x111? If so, the rampatch
   is the actual fix, and every module with this ROM should get the
   ROME setup.

Thanks,
